'''Hindustan Zinc Limited''' ('''HZL''') is an Indian integrated mining and resources producer of [[zinc]], [[lead]], [[silver]] and [[cadmium]]. It is a subsidiary of [[Vedanta Limited]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Latest Shareholding Pattern - Hindustan Zinc Ltd. |url=https://trendlyne.com/equity/share-holding/561/HINDZINC/latest/hindustan-zinc-ltd/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210814003244/https://trendlyne.com/equity/share-holding/561/HINDZINC/latest/hindustan-zinc-ltd/ |archive-date=14 August 2021 |access-date=2021-07-30 |website=[[Trendlyne]]}}</ref> Earlier it was a [[Government of India|Central Public Sector Undertaking]], sold by [[Government of India]] to [[Vedanta Limited]] when [[Atal Bihari Vajpayee]] and [[Bharatiya Janta Party]] Government was in power in the year 2003. HZL is the world's second largest zinc producer.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Beneath The Surface: How Hindustan Zinc Grew To Become The World's Second Largest Zinc Producer |url=https://www.forbesindia.com/article/super-50-companies-2017/beneath-the-surface-how-hindustan-zinc-grew-to-become-the-worlds-second-largest-zinc-producer/47649/1 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200925103420/https://www.forbesindia.com/article/super-50-companies-2017/beneath-the-surface-how-hindustan-zinc-grew-to-become-the-worlds-second-largest-zinc-producer/47649/1 |archive-date=25 September 2020 |access-date=2020-08-02 |website=[[Forbes India]] |language=en}}</ref>

==History==
{{See also|Disinvestment of Public Sector Units in India}}

Hindustan Zinc Limited was incorporated from the erstwhile Metal Corporation of India on 10 January 1966 as a [[Public sector undertakings in India|Public Sector Undertaking]] (PSU).

In 2001, as part of the [[Government of India|Government of India's]] [[Disinvestment of Public Sector Units in India|disinvestment program]] of loss-making PSUs, the company was put up for sale.<ref>{{cite web |title=Winners and losers of HZL privatisation - Times of India |url=http://articles.timesofindia.indiatimes.com/2003-02-20/india-business/27263364_1_public-sector-privatisation-labour-unions |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130103165120/http://articles.timesofindia.indiatimes.com/2003-02-20/india-business/27263364_1_public-sector-privatisation-labour-unions |archive-date=3 January 2013 |access-date=2 February 2022 |website=[[India Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sterlite seeks majority stake in Hindustan Zinc |url=https://www.rediff.com/money/2003/oct/08divest1.htm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050915041122/http://www.rediff.com/money/2003/oct/08divest1.htm |archive-date=15 September 2005 |access-date=2020-08-02 |website=[[Rediff]]}}</ref>

In April 2002, Sterlite Opportunities and Ventures Limited (SOVL) made an open offer for acquisition of shares of the company; consequent to the [[disinvestment]] of [[Government of India]]'s (GOI) stake of 26% including management control to SOVL and acquired additional 20% of shares from public, pursuant to the [[Securities and Exchange Board of India|SEBI]] Regulations 1997. In August 2003, SOVL acquired additional shares to the extent of 18.92% of the paid up capital from GOI in exercise of [[call option]] clause in the share holder's agreement between GOI and SOVL.{{Citation needed|date=April 2023}}

With the above additional acquisition, SOVL's stake in the company went up to 64.92%. Thus GOI's stake in the company now stands at 29.54%. SOVL was merged with Sterlite Industries India Ltd in April 2011.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Directors' Report FY 2013 of Sterlite Industries |url=http://profit.ndtv.com/stock/sterlite-industries-_ster/reports-directors-report |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160425041737/http://profit.ndtv.com/stock/sterlite-industries-_ster/reports-directors-report |archive-date=25 April 2016 |access-date=10 April 2016 |website=[[NDTV]]}}</ref>

Sterlite Industries merged with [[Vedanta Limited|Sesa Goa Ltd]] to form Sesa Sterlite Limited in August 2013. Sesa Sterlite was renamed to Vedanta Limited in April 2015.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.vedantalimited.com/media/83441/vedanta_limited_annual_report_with_drr_2014-15.pdf|title=Pg 77 of Vedanta Limited Annual Report FY 2015|access-date=10 April 2016|archive-date=22 April 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160422141450/http://www.vedantalimited.com/media/83441/vedanta_limited_annual_report_with_drr_2014-15.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> {{Better source needed|reason=The current source is insufficiently reliable ([[WP:NOTRS]]).|date=April 2023}} Hindustan Zinc is a direct subsidiary of [[Vedanta Limited]].

==Operations==

===Mining===
HZL operates the world's third largest [[Open-pit mining|open-pit mine]], and the world's largest zinc mine in [[Rampura Agucha]], [[Rajasthan]]. Other mines owned by HZL are located in [[Rajpura]] and [[Dariba, Rajasthan|Dariba]], Sindesar Khurd, Kayad and Zawar, all in Rajasthan.{{Citation needed|date=April 2023}}

===Smelting===
HZL operates Zinc and Lead smelters and refineries at [[Chanderiya smelter complex|Chanderiya]] (Chittorgarh), Debari ([[Udaipur]]) & Dariba ([[Rajsamand]])   in [[Rajasthan]] with total zinc and lead production capacity of 10 [[lakh]] tonnes. A smelting facility was established at  [[Pantnagar]] in [[Uttarakhand]]. It was initially intended to serve as a smelting facility for Silver production, but later Zinc and Lead melting and casting plants were also established here. The total metal production was 8,80,000 tonnes for year 2014–2015.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.hzlindia.com/common/pdf/Hindustan-Zinc-AR-2014-2015_Navigational.pdf|title=Annual Report FY 2015 Pg 11|access-date=10 April 2016|archive-date=24 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160124235148/http://www.hzlindia.com/common/pdf/Hindustan-Zinc-AR-2014-2015_Navigational.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>{{Better source needed|reason=The current source is insufficiently reliable ([[WP:NOTRS]]).|date=April 2023}} It also has zinc smelter in [[Visakhapatnam]], [[Andhra Pradesh]], where operations have been suspended since February 2012.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.hzlindia.com/common/images/HZL_AR_2012-13.pdf|title=Annual Report FY 2013 Pg 65|access-date=10 April 2016|archive-date=24 September 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130924065000/http://www.hzlindia.com/common/images/HZL_AR_2012-13.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>{{Better source needed|reason=The current source is insufficiently reliable ([[WP:NOTRS]]).|date=April 2023}}

===Sports===
[[Zinc Football|Zinc Football Academy]] is an Indian professional football academy club based in Zawar a mining town in [[Udaipur]], [[Rajasthan]]. The club competes in [[R-League A Division]], the top-tier league of the state. The club is owned by Hindustan Zinc Limited Group. Zinc FA is the current champions of [[R-League A Division 2021|R-League Season]].{{Citation needed|date=April 2023}}

